6a. WHY MIT
-----------

DoD Chief Information Officer memorandum "Software Development and Open Source
Software," 24 January 2022, Attachment 2 paragraph 3G:

  "DoD programs releasing code as OSS may use any of the following licenses,
  which have been shown to be acceptable for DoD use: Apache-2.0, BSD, GPL,
  LGPL, and MIT licenses. Component CIOs may grant permission to use other
  licenses if required."

MIT is on the approved-without-action list. It also matches SemperScribe, so the
programme carries one licence rather than two.

This is also why EUPL-licensed European public-sector software is not imported
here: EUPL is not on that list and would require Component CIO permission, on
top of the copyleft problem recorded in resources/23-eu-oss-catalogue.md.


